  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization: Core Contribution</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The International Labour Organization (ILO) is a United Nations agency dealing with labour issues, particularly international labour standards and decent work for all. The main aims of the ILO are to promote rights at work, encourage decent employment opportunities, enhance social protection and strengthen dialogue on work-related issues. Funding under this initiative represents Australia&apos;s annual subscription payment for membership and funding for ILO technical assistance programs. Members&apos; contributions are based on an assessment of the relative capacity of countries to pay. They are calculated on our proportion of world GNI (Gross National Income), then adjusted based on a range of discounts applied to developing countries. Generally speaking, ODA contributions under ILO member state core assessed funding (note: 60% of the payment constitute ODA funding) are allocated to activities such as research, analytical and statistical work, and building social dialogue.</narrative>
  </description>
